# Fetch the connected board's identity.
#
# The same information is published on the board_info topic with
# transient_local durability, so most clients never need this. It exists for a
# caller that wants the identity as a request/response rather than having to
# subscribe and wait.
#
# This is NOT a liveness probe. The driver polls CMD_BOARD_INFO on its own
# schedule and this service answers from the most recent reply; it does not
# issue a fresh request and block. Anything that needs to know whether the
# board is answering right now must read board_responding and last_frame_age on
# the GimbalStatus topic, which is what those fields are for. Claiming a live
# check here would mean a cached identity could be returned as if it had just
# been confirmed.
---
# True when a board has identified itself at least once since the driver last
# configured, and info therefore describes a real board. It does NOT mean the
# board is currently connected — a board that has since gone silent still
# reports true, with info.header.stamp showing how old the identification is.
#
# False leaves info default-constructed and message explains why. A driver that
# has never heard from the board must say so rather than return a zeroed struct
# that reads like firmware 0.0.
bool available
string message

# info.header.stamp is when the identifying reply was received, not when this
# service was called. info.header.frame_id is left empty: board identity is not
# spatial data.
BoardInfo info
